# Automated Gear-Manufacturing Process

## Technical Field

The present disclosure relates generally to the manufacture of toothed workpieces and, more particularly, to an automated process and production system for loading unmachined workpieces, generating gear teeth by hobbing at a first machining station, temporarily storing the hobbed workpieces, and deburring the hobbed workpieces at a second machining station.

## General Description

A gear-manufacturing process is carried out by an automated production system comprising a workpiece supply device, a loading apparatus, a first machining station including a hobbing machine, an intermediate storage device, a transfer apparatus, and a second machining station including a deburring machine. Operation of the production system is coordinated by at least one programmable controller.

Unmachined workpieces are initially retained in the workpiece supply device. Each workpiece may comprise a generally cylindrical gear blank having a central bore, one or more end faces, and an outer peripheral surface on which gear teeth are to be formed. The workpieces may be supplied in bulk, in trays, on pallets, or on a conveyor.

The loading apparatus automatically removes an unmachined workpiece from the supply device and transfers the workpiece to the first machining station. The loading apparatus may comprise an industrial robot, a gantry loader, a pick-and-place mechanism, or a combination thereof. Before loading, the workpiece may be identified, oriented, and inspected by one or more sensors. A gripping device of the loading apparatus engages the workpiece at the bore, outer peripheral surface, or end faces and places the workpiece in a fixture or chuck of the hobbing machine.

At the first machining station, the unmachined workpiece is clamped for rotation about a workpiece axis. A hob is brought into cutting engagement with the outer peripheral surface of the workpiece. The hob and workpiece are rotated in a synchronized relationship while relative feed motion is produced therebetween, thereby generating a plurality of circumferentially distributed gear teeth. The hobbing operation may form external spur teeth, helical teeth, splines, or another desired toothed profile. Cutting parameters, including rotational speed, feed rate, cutting depth, and synchronization ratio, are controlled according to a stored machining program.

Upon completion of the hobbing operation, the hob is withdrawn and the workpiece is released. The loading apparatus, or a separate unloading apparatus, removes the hobbed workpiece from the first machining station. Optionally, loose chips and cutting fluid are removed by air, liquid, vacuum, centrifugal action, or a combination thereof. The hobbed workpiece may also be subjected to an intermediate dimensional or visual inspection.

The hobbed workpiece is subsequently delivered to an intermediate storage device positioned functionally between the first and second machining stations. The intermediate storage device may comprise an indexed conveyor, accumulating conveyor, rotary table, rack, magazine, pallet store, or multi-position buffer. Each storage position is configured to receive and maintain a respective hobbed workpiece in a known location and, where required, in a predetermined orientation.

The intermediate storage device decouples the operating cycle of the first machining station from the operating cycle of the second machining station. Accordingly, the hobbing operation may continue when the deburring machine is temporarily unavailable, provided that storage capacity remains available. Likewise, the deburring operation may continue using previously stored workpieces during a temporary interruption of the hobbing operation.

A transfer apparatus retrieves a selected hobbed workpiece from the intermediate storage device and conveys the selected workpiece to the second machining station. Selection may be performed on a first-in, first-out basis or according to production priority, workpiece type, elapsed storage time, inspection status, or machine availability. Workpiece identification data may be associated with each storage position or carried by an identification element assigned to the workpiece.

At the second machining station, the hobbed workpiece is automatically inserted into and secured by a fixture of the deburring machine. The deburring machine removes burrs produced along tooth edges during the hobbing operation. Deburring may be performed mechanically by one or more rotating brushes, cutting tools, grinding tools, scraping tools, or chamfering tools. Alternatively or additionally, the deburring operation may employ abrasive flow, thermal deburring, electrochemical treatment, or another edge-finishing technique.

During mechanical deburring, relative movement is produced between the deburring tool and the workpiece such that at least one axial end region of each gear tooth is contacted. The workpiece may be rotated while the deburring tool is advanced along or across the tooth edges. Both axial end faces may be processed sequentially or concurrently. The deburring operation may further form a controlled chamfer or edge break at one or more tooth edges.

After deburring, the finished workpiece is unclamped and removed automatically from the second machining station. The workpiece may then be transferred to an inspection station, washing station, heat-treatment process, packaging station, or finished-part storage area. A nonconforming workpiece may be diverted to a rejection location or rework station.

## Process Control

The programmable controller receives operating-state information from the first machining station, intermediate storage device, transfer apparatus, and second machining station. Based on such information, the controller coordinates loading, hobbing, storage, retrieval, and deburring operations while preventing conflicting movements.

The controller may maintain an electronic record for each workpiece, the record including one or more of:

- a workpiece identifier;
- a workpiece type or production order;
- hobbing parameters;
- time of completion of the hobbing operation;
- an assigned intermediate storage position;
- inspection results;
- deburring parameters; and
- completion or rejection status.

Sensors may verify workpiece presence, orientation, clamping, tool position, storage occupancy, and transfer completion. If the intermediate storage device reaches a predetermined occupancy threshold, the controller may reduce or suspend loading of the hobbing machine. If the second machining station is available, the controller may prioritize removal of a stored workpiece to maintain continuous production.

## Exemplary Method

In an exemplary embodiment, the automated process comprises:

1. providing a plurality of unmachined gear workpieces at a supply location;
2. automatically selecting and orienting a respective unmachined workpiece;
3. transferring the workpiece to a hobbing machine at a first machining station;
4. clamping the workpiece and generating gear teeth by synchronized rotation of the workpiece and a hob;
5. unloading the resulting hobbed workpiece from the first machining station;
6. transferring the hobbed workpiece to an available position of an intermediate storage device;
7. retaining the hobbed workpiece in the intermediate storage device until the second machining station is available;
8. retrieving the hobbed workpiece from the intermediate storage device;
9. transferring and loading the hobbed workpiece into a deburring machine at the second machining station;
10. removing burrs from at least one edge of the hobbed gear teeth; and
11. unloading the deburred workpiece for subsequent processing or storage.

The intermediate storage device thereby permits substantially independent cycling of the hobbing and deburring stations while preserving automated workpiece flow between the stations. The disclosed arrangement can improve equipment utilization, accommodate differences in machining cycle time, and reduce manual handling of workpieces.
